
---
import MainLayout from "../../layouts/MainLayout.astro";
---

<MainLayout
  title="Ratemaking | Eddie Zhu"
  description="Ratemaking"
>

  <!-- PROJECT INTRODUCTION -->

  <section class="project-hero">
    <div class="container">

      <a href="/projects" class="back-link">
        ← Back to Projects
      </a>

      <p class="eyebrow">Insurance Ratemaking</p>

      <h1>Insurance Pricing using Generalized Linear Models (GLMs)</h1>

      <p class="subtitle">
        An end-to-end actuarial pricing workflow using the norauto motor 
        insurance dataset from the CASdatasets package, to estimate the expected 
        claim cost for each policyholder through Frequency, Severity, Pure Premium, Gross Premium.
      </p>

      <div class="technologies">
        <span>Python</span>
        <span>R</span>
        <span>GLM</span>
      </div>

      <a
        href="https://github.com/Eddie-Zhu-IRL/Motor-Insurance-Pricing"
        target="_blank"
        rel="noopener noreferrer"
        class="button"
      >
        View GitHub Repository ↗
      </a>

    </div>
  </section>


  <!-- PROJECT OBJECTIVE -->

  <section class="section">
    <div class="container">

      <h2>1. Project Objective</h2>

      <p>
        This project demonstrates an end-to-end actuarial pricing workflow using the norauto motor insurance dataset from the CASdatasets package.
      </p>

      <p>
        The objective is to estimate the expected claim cost for each policyholder by modelling:
        <ul>
          <li>Frequency</li>
          <li>Severity</li>
          <li>Pure Premium</li>
          <li>Gross Premium</li>
        </ul>
      </p>

    </div>
  </section>


  <!-- METHODOLOGY -->

  <section class="section alternate">
    <div class="container">

      <h2>2. Actuarial Methodology</h2>

      <p>
        Motor insurers must determine premiums that adequately reflect each policyholder's 
        expected future claims while covering operating expenses and generating a target level of profit.
      </p>

      <p>
        Generalized Linear Models (GLMs) is used to estimate expected claim costs and convert them into insurance premiums. 
        Gross premiums are estimated by incorporating expense loadings and target profit margins into the expected claim costs.
      </p>

      <div class="method-grid">

        <article>
          <h3>EDA & Feature Engineering</h3>
          <ul>
            <li>Feature Engineering: relevel factor variables</li>
            <li>Data Summary: frequency and severity on independent variables</li>
          </ul>
        </article>

        <article>
          <h3>Frequency Modelling</h3>
          <ul>
            <li>Poisson Regression</li>
            <li>Negative Binomial Regression</li>
          </ul>
        </article>

        <article>
          <h3>Severity Modelling</h3>
          <ul>
            <li>Gamma GLM</li>
            <li>Lognormal GLM</li>
          </ul>
        </article>

        <article>
          <h3>Pure Premium & Gross Premium</h3>
          <ul>
            <li>Pure Premium = Frequency * Severity</li>
            <li>Gross Premium = Pure Premium / Permissible Loss Ratio</li>
          </ul>
        </article>

      </div>

    </div>
  </section>


  <!-- IMPLEMENTATION -->

  <section class="section">
    <div class="container">

      <h2>3. Technical Implementation</h2>

      <div class="implementation-grid">

        <article>
          <h3>GLM</h3>
          <p>
            Modelling:
          <ul>
            <li>Frequency - Poisson, Negative Binomial</li>
            <li>Severity - Gamma, LogNormal</li>
          </ul>
          </p>

          <p>
            Metric: AIC, BIC, MSE
          </p>
        </article>

        <article>
          <h3>R</h3>
          <p>
            Data processing, visualization and GLM using following libraries:
            <ul>
              <li>stats</li>
              <li>MASS</li>
              <li>tidyverse</li>
            </ul>
          </p>
        </article>

        <article>
          <h3>Python</h3>
          <p>
            Data processing, visualization and GLM using following packages.
            <ul>
              <li>pandas</li>
              <li>matplotlib</li>
              <li>statsmodels</li>
            </ul>
          </p>
        </article>

      </div>

    </div>
  </section>


  <!-- VALIDATION -->

  <section class="section alternate">
    <div class="container">

      <h2>4. Summary and Output Visuals</h2>

      <p>
        The predicted aggregate pure premium was approximately 2.8% higher than the observed portfolio loss, 
        indicating good agreement between the selected frequency and severity models by R. Model by R 
        over-estimates loss while model by Python under-estimates loss by 0.4%. A gap of this size is 
        consistent with the two languages using different random splits, since claim counts and large 
        losses in a 20% test set vary from split to split.
      </p>
      <img
      src="/images/pricing/headline_results.png"
      alt="headline_results"
      class="headline_results"
      />

      <p>
        The pure premium represents the expected claim cost only. In practice, insurers must also account 
        for operating expenses, commissions, reinsurance costs, regulatory requirements, and target profit 
        when determining the final premium charged to customers. Gross premium was estimated by applying a 
        permissible loss ratio assumption of 70%, implying that expected claims represent 70% of the premium collected.
      </p>
      
      <p>
        For the R model, a mean pure premium of €1,132 and a gross premium of €1,617 per test policy.
      </p>
      
      <ul>
        <li>R Output</li>
        <img
        src="/images/pricing/summary_r.png"
        alt="summary_r"
        class="summary_r"
        />

        <li>Python Output</li>
        <img
        src="/images/pricing/summary_python.png"
        alt="summary_python"
        class="summary_python"
        />
      </ul>

      <p>
        Predicted Pure Premium vs Actual Loss by Gender, Young_26YO, DistLimit and GeoRegion is plotted using R and Python.
        Differences in final premium estimates were caused by different random train/test partitions between R and 
        Python rather than modelling differences.
      </p>
      
      <ul>
        <li>R Visuals</li>
        <img
        src="/images/pricing/pred_act_r.png"
        alt="pred_act_r"
        class="pred_act_r"
        />

        <li>Python Visuals</li>
        <img
        src="/images/pricing/pred_act_python.png"
        alt="pred_act_python"
        class="pred_act_python"
        />
      </ul>

    </div>
  </section>


  <!-- LEARNING -->

  <section class="section">
    <div class="container">

      <h2>5. Skills Developed</h2>

      <div class="skills-list">
        <span>Python</span>
        <span>R</span>
        <span>Ratemaking</span>
        <span>Frequency</span>
        <span>Severity</span>
        <span>Pure Premium</span>
        <span>Gross Premium</span>
        <span>Visualization</span>
        <span>Validation</span>
        <span>Actuarial Analysis</span>
      </div>

    </div>
  </section>


  <!-- GITHUB -->

  <section class="project-contact">
    <div class="container">

      <h2>Explore the Project</h2>

      <p>
        View the ratemaking model, Python and R scripts, calculations
        and technical documentation on GitHub.
      </p>

      <a
        href="https://github.com/Eddie-Zhu-IRL/Motor-Insurance-Pricing"
        target="_blank"
        rel="noopener noreferrer"
        class="button"
      >
        Visit GitHub Repository ↗
      </a>

    </div>
  </section>

</MainLayout>


<style>
  .project-hero {
    background: var(--color-navy);
    color: white;
    padding: 75px 0;
  }

  .back-link {
    color: #BFDBFE;
    display: inline-block;
    margin-bottom: 35px;
  }

  .eyebrow {
    color: #93C5FD;
    font-weight: 700;
    letter-spacing: 1px;
  }

  .project-hero h1 {
    font-size: clamp(2rem, 5vw, 3.5rem);
    max-width: 850px;
  }

  .subtitle {
    max-width: 750px;
    color: #DCE6F3;
    font-size: 1.1rem;
    line-height: 1.7;
  }

  .technologies,
  .skills-list {
    display: flex;
    flex-wrap: wrap;
    gap: 10px;
    margin: 25px 0;
  }

  .technologies span,
  .skills-list span {
    background: #EFF6FF;
    color: #1D4ED8;
    padding: 7px 12px;
    border-radius: 5px;
    font-size: 0.85rem;
  }

  .method-grid,
  .implementation-grid {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: repeat(2, 1fr);
    gap: 20px;
    margin-top: 30px;
  }

  .method-grid article,
  .implementation-grid article {
    background: white;
    border: 1px solid var(--color-border);
    border-radius: 8px;
    padding: 22px;
  }

  h2 {
    color: var(--color-navy);
    font-size: 1.8rem;
  }

  h3 {
    color: var(--color-navy);
  }

  .alternate {
    background: #EDF2F8;
  }

  .section p,
  .section li {
    color: var(--color-muted);
    line-height: 1.8;
  }

  .project-contact {
    background: var(--color-navy);
    color: white;
    text-align: center;
    padding: 65px 0;
  }

  .project-contact h2 {
    color: white;
  }

  .project-contact p {
    color: #DCE6F3;
  }

  @media (max-width: 650px) {
    .method-grid,
    .implementation-grid {
      grid-template-columns: 1fr;
    }
  }
</style>
